--- ## A CPU probe under a mux, driven from IXQL
--- Declares and starts a mux, declares and starts a CPU probe feeding it, reads ten of the
--- mux's records from the plane's ring, then removes the tree. One IXQL capability per probe
--- op, named after the wire's (`ix.ctl.probe.create`, `.start`, …); samples are read with
--- `ix.ctl.events`, the capability every pipe is read with (D7, ix#405).
---
---   ixctl up spec.json          -- any plane: probes need no service
---   cargo run -p ix-ctl --example ixql-ctl -- crates/ix-ctl/examples/probe.ixql --state <state file>
---
--- The runner prints every binding below as JSON.

--- What this plane can sample, and the limits its tree holds a node to: a kind that is not
--- listed is a refusal to make here, before any node exists.
kinds <- ix.ctl.probe.kinds()

--- The mux first, so the leaf's `parent:` names a node that exists, and started at once: the
--- lease (`ttl_ms`) counts only on a running node, and it is the cleanup. IXQL has no `finally`,
--- a step that fails ends the run, and a program that dies leaves a tree the plane removes by
--- itself when the lease runs out (`probe.removed`, reason `expired`); `probe.touch` would renew
--- it. Started before the leaf, the mux also carries the leaf's first samples: a mux carries
--- only while running, else they stay on `probe/cpu` alone.
agg <- ix.ctl.probe.create(probe: { name: "agg", kind: "mux@1", ttl_ms: 30000 })
agg_on <- ix.ctl.probe.start(node: "agg")

--- The leaf under it: a listed kind, its own interval, a filter that keeps one field. A create
--- the plane refuses ends the run here, and the lease above takes the mux with it in time.
cpu <- ix.ctl.probe.create(probe: {
    name: "cpu", kind: "cpu@1", every_ms: 100, parent: "agg",
    filter: { keep: ["busy_pct"] }
  })
cpu_on <- ix.ctl.probe.start(node: "cpu")

--- Ten records as the mux publishes them (`src: "cpu"`), live: without `since:` the read sees
--- what comes after the stream opens, which here is everything, since `cpu@1` emits nothing on
--- its first interval. A replay from the start of the boot (`since: 0, epoch: <the status's>`)
--- would give a node's first records even when the stream opened late, but it would also give
--- the records of an earlier tree of the same name in this boot: the pipe is the name's, not
--- the node's. The read stops at `max:` records or after `timeout_ms` without a next one: fewer
--- than ten events in the answer is a partial read, and a `gap` says the ring has turned or the
--- plane rebooted. A read error (`{…, error}`) is a record too, whatever the filter, and counts.
samples <- ix.ctl.events(channel: "probe/agg", max: 10, timeout_ms: 3000)

--- The whole tree: `cpu` goes with reason `parent-removed`, `removed` counts 2.
gone <- ix.ctl.probe.remove(node: "agg")
